# Trivy suppression list. Every scan reads this same file explicitly, so a
# suppression added here applies to all of them:
#   • .github/workflows/lint.yml         → `trivy fs` over the source tree
#                                          (runs on every code PR)
#   • .github/workflows/container-ci.yml → `trivy image`, via the shared
#                                          .github/actions/scan-image action:
#                                          the `image-scan` job scans the
#                                          ci-<sha>/fork image on every code
#                                          PR (#596), and the `build` job
#                                          scans the published :<version>
#                                          image on release PRs
#
# It is currently empty on purpose — every finding it used to hold is now fixed
# at the source rather than hidden, and as of the severity-gate tightening the
# whole image scans clean (zero findings at every severity).
#
# The bar for adding an entry went UP, not down, when the gate moved from
# "CRITICAL only" to "CRITICAL + fixable HIGH/MEDIUM". A lower threshold trips
# more often, which is exactly when suppression starts to look like the quick
# fix — so read this before reaching for it:
#
#   • A finding with NO released fix does not need an entry at all. The gate
#     already treats unfixable HIGH/MEDIUM as advisory (it blocks only on
#     findings that name a FixedVersion), precisely so nobody has to suppress a
#     CVE they cannot fix. If a scan is red, upstream has shipped a fix and the
#     honest move is to take it.
#   • An entry silences a finding for BOTH gates and for every future PR, with
#     nothing to make anyone revisit it. That is the cost.
#
# So an entry is justified only where a fix exists but this repo demonstrably
# cannot take it (e.g. a transitive pin the add-on's base image controls). Say
# which of those it is, and what would let the entry be deleted.
#
# History: CVE-2024-45337 and CVE-2025-68121 were suppressed here because
# /usr/bin/tempio — a Go binary bundled by ghcr.io/home-assistant/base-python,
# owned by no apk package — was built from go1.23.3 + golang.org/x/crypto
# v0.26.0, so nothing this repo could do would patch it. Both entries are now
# moot twice over: Home Assistant rebuilt tempio in 2026-07 (go1.26.5,
# x/crypto v0.31.0 — past both fixes), and smart_vent/Dockerfile now deletes the
# binary outright because Plenum never invokes it.
#
# Before adding an entry, check whether the finding can be FIXED instead:
#   • an OS package → add an apk version floor to smart_vent/Dockerfile (the
#     floors there are also what stops BuildKit re-serving a stale upgrade
#     layer — see the comment above them);
#   • a base-image file Plenum does not use → delete it in the Dockerfile;
#   • a Python/npm dependency → bump it in pyproject.toml / package.json.
# Suppress only what none of those can reach, and say why in a comment.
